  "client_view_text": " A write-ahead log is a file that contains a record of all changes made to the database. When a change is made to A write-ahead log is a file that contains a record of all changes made to the database. When a change is made to the database, the change is first written to the log, and then the change is written to the database itself. This ensures that the log always contains a complete and accurate record of all changes made to the database, even if the database itself is corrupted or lost.\nWhen a database crashes, the write-ahead log can be used to recover the database to a consistent state. The log is scanned from the end, and each change is reapplied to the database in the order in which it was written. This ensures that the database is restored to a consistent state, even if the database itself is corrupted or lost. The log can also be used to recover from a partial failure, where the database is partially corrupted but still accessible. In this case, the log can be used to identify the point at which the failure occurred and to recover the database to that point.\nThe write-ahead log also provides a way to recover from a situation where the database is inconsistent, such as when a transaction is partially committed but not fully committed. In this case, the log can be used to identify the point at which the transaction was started and to roll back the transaction to that"
